# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# winoracle 2026-09-28, WARP ("Microsoft Basic Render Driver"), x64 above.
# The i686 build prints the same bytes except "pointer size 4" and the three
# isoline lines' PSInvocations/occlusion columns: WARP's own coverage of those
# tessellated lines differs between its x86 and x64 builds (x86: occ 255 /
# 390 / 395); every other counter agrees.
# What WARP shows where D3D11.3 20.4.7 leaves a range (the rules Vitreo
# reproduces, graphics/shaders/pipeline_stats.hpp):
#   VSInvocations  distinct vertices per batch of 512 / (vertices per
#                  primitive) assembled primitives (170 triangles, 256 lines,
#                  512 points, 85 triangles with adjacency) per instance; a
#                  spare vertex no primitive uses is not shaded; a strip
#                  re-shades the two vertices a batch boundary splits.
#   CPrimitives    Sutherland-Hodgman against -w<=x,y<=w, 0<=z<=w (z only
#                  with DepthClipEnable) and SV_ClipDistance, inside when
#                  >= 0, coincident vertices kept (a fullscreen triangle
#                  through the viewport corner is 3); no guard band.
#   CInvocations   every primitive reaching the clipper (degenerate and
#                  back-facing ones too).
#   DSInvocations  every vertex of every output primitive (3 a triangle, 2 an
#                  isoline segment -- no reuse); unique points for point
#                  output. GSPrimitives counts every stream.
#   PSInvocations  every lane of each 2x2 stamp holding a covered pixel
#                  (helpers included), whatever the depth/stencil result or a
#                  discard; [earlydepthstencil] stamps whose pixels all fail
#                  are not shaded. At 4x, pixel and sample frequency alike,
#                  pixel stamps again (4224 for the full quad).
# Verdict for Vitreo (pstats pass, 2026-09-28): 186 -> 47 differing lines
# (x64 and x86), every one in the PSInvocations column alone. On 44 the
# OCCLUSION column is equal -- the same pixels are covered, and only helper
# pixels differ (D3D11.3 20.4.7: PSInvocations "may include or exclude helper
# pixels"); the other 3 are the isolines, whose coverage differs (see above:
# WARP disagrees with itself across builds there):
#   - 4x MSAA (12 lines): Vitreo counts each covered pixel once, helpers
#     excluded (Metal runs a memory-writing fragment function per sample and
#     its helper lanes carry no coverage, so one stamp's invocations cannot
#     be joined): 4160 where WARP counts 4224.
#   - lines (5), clipped or viewport-crossing triangles and quads (18),
#     tessellated triangles (6): WARP rasterizes the clipper's fan or its own
#     line triangles, whose internal edges add helper stamps; Metal's
#     rasterizer splits differently (a guard band) -- e.g. one vertex past +x
#     796 / 784, the fullscreen triangle 4224 / 4232.
#   - a scissor rectangle at an odd offset (1): 112 / 104.
#   - the DepthClipEnable FALSE triangles (2): 576 / 640 (Metal's depth clamp).
